This is probably the single biggest structural lesson to take from the reference UI.

5. Use prominent CTA buttons consistently — P1
Adopt a clear primary/secondary button hierarchy.
Primary: Add to Cart / Buy Now
Secondary: View Details / Browse Category
Avoid having many competing CTAs with equal visual weight.

6. Stronger trust section — P1
The reference UI demonstrates how services can be made reassuring through concise benefit statements.
For MeatSoko, trust should be product-specific, not generic:
Where the meat comes from
Quality standards
Freshness
Packaging
Delivery handling
Payment security
Returns/refunds
Customer support
This directly addresses one of the major weaknesses identified in our audit.

7. Better "Why MeatSoko?" section — P1
Add a concise section explaining why someone should buy from MeatSoko rather than a conventional butcher/market.
Potential structure:
Why MeatSoko?
Quality
Know what you're buying.
Convenience
Order from wherever you are.
Reliable delivery
Know when your order is arriving.
Trusted marketplace
Clear sellers, products and support.
The actual claims should be validated by the business.

8. Cleaner category presentation — P1
The reference approach favours visually digestible sections rather than exposing everything simultaneously.
MeatSoko should avoid presenting dozens of categories at once.
Instead:
Popular Categories
[Beef] [Goat & Mutton] [Chicken] [Minced] [Offal]
Explore Agriculture
[Livestock] [Feeds] [Veterinary] [Equipment] [Dairy]
This would substantially reduce cognitive load.

